From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Date: Wed, 20 Apr 1994 21:28:35 -0100
Subject: [#94-4-153] Life Extension

Brian Rauchfuss writes:
>There is a drug which is reported to increase the limited number of times
>cells can divide, which has important implications for life-extension.
>

Can you provide more details?  This sounds cool.

Begin rampant speculation mode:

It seems to me that any such drug would substantially increase the risk of
cancer.  

As I understand it, the limit to the number of cell divisions is regulated
by the length of repeating segments of DNA on the end of chromosomes,
called telomeres.  After each cell division, the new chromosomes have one
less unit in their telomeres.  When the length of telomeres goes to 0, the
cell can no longer divide.  This process is mediated by an enzyme called
telomerase.  The presumed purpose of this system is to prevent unlimited
reproduction of cells, i.e. cancer, should some other part of the
regulatory machinery of the cell get screwed up and cause erroneous
repeated division.  

Presumably a drug that blocked telomerase would prevent shortening of the
telomeres.  But this would remove one of the protective systems against
cancer, thereby increasing its risk, espescially for older people.  I
wonder if such a drug would be a net win?  Perhaps it would be, since we
have some reasonably good ways of curing cancer nowadays.

Of course, the FDA would never allow it, since it would prevent old age,
which is not a disease, but cause cancer, which is.  

(Warning: I am not a biologist.  The preceding message is based on vague
recollection of articles in Science and Nature.  Correction by others is
welcome.)


--carlf

