From: (Ronald C)
To: dcm
Subject: They're Herrreeeee

Did you catch the article in the New York Times about the Arizona lawyer
that was advertising on netnews?  He hit every group with an ad for his
services and was promply mail bombed with 30k+ messages.  His service
provider's system crashed (repeatedly) due to the load, eventually causing
them to revoke the lawyer's account.  Our friend responded by hitting the
service provider with a $250k suit -- the estimated advertising value of
Internet access.

The lawyer tried the same stunt on a smaller scale on two other service
provider's systems, had similar results, and lost both accounts.  The third
time was the biggest and best ever, and it apparently produced a few live
leads, so he's now convinced that Internet advertising is the wave of the
future (never mind the occasional nasty mail message).  He said he hopes to
write a book on how to advertise on the Internet.

They lawyer's contention is that "Hey -- it's a public forum.  You can do
whatever you want."  The really unfortunate thing is that he's right, and
he is perfectly willing to use his legal resources to push for what he can
do rather than what he should do.  The Internet is definately reaching
mainstream, and there's many aspects of this new-found popularity that I
really don't like.  It is time to start developing smart agents to filter
mail and news in order to avoid the junk.






