ZEDILLO SAYS DEMOCRACY KEY TO MEXICAN ECONOMIC FUTURE

Ernesto Zedillo Ponce de Leon, the presidential candidate of Mexico's
long-governing PRI party, says democratic reforms are the key to
making economic and social progress in Mexico.  "I do not believe the
country can advance significantly in the economic or social areas if
we do not strengthen our democracy," Zedillo said in an interview
Saturday.

But Zedillo has not endorsed any measures to open the electoral
process that go further than the plans being negotiated by the main
parties.  Many PRI opponents claim the only way Zedillo can win the
presidency is through the kind of fraud the PRI has been accused of
in the past.

Sources: Tim Golden, "More Democracy Vital, Mexican Candidate
Says," NEW YORK TIMES, April 18, 1994; Sallie Hughes, "Me and My
Shadow," EL FINANCIERO INTERNATIONAL, April 11-17, 1994.
_____________________________________________
NAFTA HURTING FLORIDA FARMERS

Even though the North American Free Trade Agreement has only
been in effect for three months, Florida tomato and vegetable
farmers say they are already suffering from a flood of Mexican
imports.

"The market has been horrible," said Steve Sapp, owner of D&S Farms
in Homestead, Florida.  With two months left in the growing season,
Mexican farmers have exported about 483 million pounds of
tomatoes to the U.S. this year.  Last year Mexico exported a total of
466 million pounds for the entire season.  Farmers say Mexico is
shipping the produce below the cost of U.S. production.

Federal officials deny the increase is the result of NAFTA.  "There are
things that are happening in the world that are going to affect the
supply and prices of vegetables in Florida, but I don't think NAFTA is
an important factor of that," said Keith Collins, acting assistant
secretary of economics at the U.S. Department of Agriculture.

Source: Oscar Musibay, "Florida's South Dade Farmers Say the NAFTA
Is Already Causing Pain," JOURNAL OF COMMERCE, April 14, 1994.
_____________________________________________
ILWU WILL FIGHT FOR MEXICAN RIGHTS

The International Longshoremen's and Warehousemen's Union
(ILWU) vowed to support dockworkers in Mexico and Asia if workers
rights are violated.  At last week's triennial convention in Los
Angeles, the union unanimously passed a resolution stating that the
ILWU will take "any appropriate action necessary" if "union busting"
measures continue in Mexico.

Jack Heyman, an ILWU delegate from San Francisco, said Mexico's
port privatization program is a disguised union-busting operation.
The plan replaces government-owned operations with private-sector
enterprises, leaving dockworker contracts voided.  The dockworkers
then must renegotiate new contracts at substantially lower pay.
Heyman said dockworkers who used to earn $8 an hour are now
making $1 an hour.

The ILWU expects such practices to increase in frequency.  "If you're
worried about NAFTA, GATT will make it look like a tea party,"
Heyman said.  "The board of directors at GATT will determine what is
a barrier to trade," he said.  "Union contracts will be thrown out.
Workers' bills of rights will be thrown out."

Heyman said the ILWU would consider a coastwide strike to support
Mexican workers if such activities continue.  ILWU's statements were
applauded by dockworker delegations from Mexico, Japan and
Singapore.

Source: Bill Mongelluzzo, "ILWU Vows to Fight for Rights of Mexican,
Asian Workers," JOURNAL OF COMMERCE, April 15, 1994.
_____________________________________________
SLOWER GROWTH FOR MEXICO PREDICTED

Prior to passage and implementation of NAFTA, most analysts
predicted the trade agreement would lead to quick economic growth
for Mexico.  Many are now revising their forecasts.  Estimates at the
end of 1993 predicted Mexico's gross domestic product would
increase by more than 3 percent.  Those figures are now being
decreased to between 2 percent and 2.5 percent.

Source: Rossanna Fuentes-Berain, "Analysts See Slower Growth for
Mexico," EL FINANCIERO INTERNATIONAL, April 11-17, 1994.
_____________________________________________
MEXICO WILL ALLOW U.S. TRUCKS

Major trucking companies from Canada, the United States and Mexico
gathered in Scottsdale, Arizona last week for their first meeting since
NAFTA went into effect.

"This conference is focussing on some of the practical applications of
the changes that the NAFTA framework brings about for trucking,"
said Linda Bauer-Darr, vice-president of international affairs for the
American Trucking Association.  The week's sessions included
speeches on Mexican economic and safety issues, as well as seminars
on U.S. regulatory issues, North American harmonization and cross-
border documentation.

The U.S. trucking industry also strongly criticized a Mexican decision
to allow only Canadian truckers across the border.  But Mexico is
expected to announce it will allow U.S. truckers to cross the border to
deliver and pick up freight.

"I don't think there's any question that we're going to have the same
opportunity as the Canadians to get into Mexico," said ATA President
Thomas J. Donohue.

Source: Kevin G. Hall, "Mexico Likely to Allow U.S. Trucks Across the
Border, Official Says," JOURNAL OF COMMERCE, April 15, 1994.
_____________________________________________
SD TRADE MISSION TO MEXICO

South Dakota Governor Walter Miller and representatives of six
South Dakota companies traveled to Mexico Saturday as part of a
three-day trade mission designed to increase U.S. exports.  "Mexico
continues to be a key market for South Dakota," Miller said before
leaving.  "In the last two years alone, Mexico has gone from being our
ninth largest to our fifth largest market."

Miller was scheduled to meet with President Carlos Salinas de Gortari
and members of his cabinet.  The business representatives arranged
one-on-one meetings with Mexican companies.  Four of the six
businesses will then travel with state economic development officials
to Venezuela for a trade mission there.

North Dakota is also trying to market its products to Latin America.
A North Dakota representative will attend the Latin America
Catalogue Trade Show June 20-July 1 and spend time in Mexico,
Brazil, Peru and Ecuador.

Sources: "Trade Across the Border," AG WEEK, April 11, 1994; "N.D.
Products to Be Marketed in Latin America," AG WEEK, April 11, 1994.
